(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-endo-iii-exam1-male-reproductive"]={"title":"Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which hypothalamic hormone initiates male reproductive hormone secretion?","choices":["GHRH","CRH","GnRH","TRH"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. GnRH (gonadotropin-releasing hormone) from the hypothalamus sits at the top of the reproductive axis, driving anterior pituitary release of LH and FSH. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"LH primarily stimulates which cells?","choices":["Sertoli cells","Theca cells","Granulosa cells","Leydig cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. LH acts on Leydig cells (interstitial cells) to stimulate testosterone production. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The primary function of Leydig cells is:","choices":["Inhibin secretion","Testosterone production","Estrogen production","Spermatogenesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Leydig cells are the testosterone factories of the testis, converting cholesterol into testosterone under LH stimulation. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"FSH primarily stimulates:","choices":["Leydig cells","Corpus luteum","Adrenal cortex","Sertoli cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. FSH targets Sertoli cells to support spermatogenesis and stimulate inhibin secretion. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone is produced by Sertoli cells to inhibit FSH secretion?","choices":["DHT","Testosterone","Progesterone","Inhibin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inhibin, secreted by Sertoli cells, selectively feeds back to suppress FSH release from the pituitary. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The sequence of hormonal regulation in the male reproductive axis is:","choices":["Testosterone → GnRH → LH","FSH → GnRH → Testosterone","GnRH → LH/FSH → Testes","LH → GnRH → Testosterone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The axis flows GnRH (hypothalamus) → LH/FSH (anterior pituitary) → testes, which then produce testosterone and support spermatogenesis. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Testosterone is synthesized primarily from:","choices":["Tryptophan","Tyrosine","Glycine","Cholesterol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Like all steroid hormones, testosterone is synthesized from cholesterol through a series of enzymatic conversions in Leydig cells. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Most circulating testosterone is:","choices":["Bound only to albumin","Bound to plasma proteins","Free","Stored inside RBCs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. About 98% of testosterone circulates bound to plasma proteins (mainly SHBG and albumin), leaving only a small free fraction that is biologically active. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of testosterone circulates unbound?","choices":["2%","25%","10%","50%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Roughly 2% of testosterone is unbound/free — the fraction that can enter cells and act — while the rest is protein-bound. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Which enzyme converts testosterone into DHT?","choices":["Aromatase","Cyclooxygenase","5α-reductase","Deiodinase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. 5α-reductase converts testosterone to the more potent androgen DHT in peripheral target tissues. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Dihydrotestosterone (DHT) is:","choices":["Secreted by the pituitary","Less potent than testosterone","An estrogen","More potent than testosterone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. DHT binds the androgen receptor more tightly than testosterone, making it more potent; it drives external genitalia development and pattern baldness. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone is responsible for development of external male genitalia during fetal life?","choices":["DHT","Progesterone","Inhibin","Estradiol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. DHT is required for development of the external male genitalia in utero; testosterone itself drives the internal (Wolffian) structures, while DHT handles the external ones. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Which gene initiates testicular differentiation in the embryo?","choices":["SRY","SOX9","BRCA","p53"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The SRY gene on the Y chromosome triggers testis differentiation; without SRY, the default developmental pathway is female. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) stimulates fetal:","choices":["Theca cells","Granulosa cells","Sertoli cells","Leydig cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fetal (placental) hCG stimulates fetal Leydig cells to make testosterone before the fetal pituitary axis matures, mirroring LH's later role. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Which is considered a primary male sexual characteristic?","choices":["Deepened voice","Beard growth","Penis enlargement","Increased muscle mass"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Primary sexual characteristics are the reproductive organs themselves, and penis enlargement/development falls into this category. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Which is a secondary male sexual characteristic?","choices":["Testes","Penis","Increased muscle mass","Vas deferens"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Increased muscle mass is a secondary sexual characteristic driven by testosterone's anabolic effects at puberty. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Testosterone increases:","choices":["Bone density","Osteoclast activity","Plasma glucose only","Fat deposition"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Testosterone is anabolic to bone, increasing bone density, partly via aromatization to estrogen. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Testosterone increases erythropoiesis primarily by:","choices":["Decreasing erythropoietin","Increasing RBC production","Lowering hematocrit","Destroying RBCs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Testosterone stimulates erythropoietin and directly boosts RBC production, raising hematocrit — which is why androgen excess can cause polycythemia. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"During puberty testosterone increases:","choices":["Basal metabolic rate","Body temperature only","Thyroxine secretion","Insulin secretion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Testosterone raises basal metabolic rate during puberty through its anabolic, tissue-building effects. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Male-pattern baldness results primarily from:","choices":["Estrogen deficiency","Low LH","Cortisol","DHT plus genetic predisposition"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Male-pattern baldness requires both DHT acting on hair follicles and a genetic predisposition to DHT sensitivity in those follicles. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Spermatogenesis occurs within the:","choices":["Epididymis","Prostate","Vas deferens","Seminiferous tubules"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Spermatogenesis takes place in the seminiferous tubules of the testis, supported by Sertoli cells. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"After Meiosis I, germ cells become:","choices":["Haploid","Polyploid","Tetraploid","Diploid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Meiosis I is the reductional division: diploid primary spermatocytes become haploid secondary spermatocytes. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Spermiogenesis refers to:","choices":["Maturation of spermatids into spermatozoa","Fertilization","Mitosis","Meiosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Spermiogenesis is the final maturation of round spermatids into streamlined spermatozoa (forming the acrosome, flagellum, and condensed nucleus) — not a cell division. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Most sperm are stored in the:","choices":["Seminal vesicles","Seminiferous tubules","Epididymis","Prostate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The epididymis is the main storage and maturation site for sperm, where they gain motility before ejaculation. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The epididymal environment is normally:","choices":["Strongly alkaline","Neutral","Hypertonic only","Acidic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The epididymal lumen is mildly acidic, which keeps stored sperm quiescent (immotile) until ejaculation. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Capacitation occurs:","choices":["After ejaculation in the female reproductive tract","Inside the epididymis","During meiosis","Inside the testes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Capacitation — the final functional maturation enabling fertilization — occurs in the female reproductive tract after ejaculation, not in the testes or epididymis. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The acrosome contains enzymes that allow sperm to:","choices":["Produce testosterone","Undergo meiosis","Penetrate the ovum","Swim faster"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The acrosome caps the sperm head and contains hydrolytic enzymes (e.g., hyaluronidase, acrosin) that digest the zona pellucida so the sperm can penetrate the ovum. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Which gland contributes the largest percentage of semen volume?","choices":["Prostate","Seminal vesicles","Testes","Bulbourethral glands"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The seminal vesicles contribute the largest share of semen volume (roughly 60–70%), rich in fructose. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Parasympathetic stimulation primarily causes:","choices":["Erection","Ejaculation","Emission","Orgasm only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Parasympathetic input drives erection ('Point and Shoot' mnemonic: Parasympathetic = erection, Sympathetic = ejaculation). — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Which sequence is correct?","choices":["GnRH → LH → Leydig → Testosterone","GnRH → FSH → Leydig → Testosterone","GnRH → LH → Sertoli → Testosterone","GnRH → FSH → Prostate → Testosterone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The integrated axis is GnRH → LH → Leydig cells → testosterone; FSH acts on Sertoli cells, not Leydig, so the LH pathway is the testosterone route. — Endocrine III — Male Reproductive Physiology lecture"}]};
